  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "Unknown whether the barbiturate-glycine acid is a substrate for intestinal uptake transporters (e.g., PEPT1, OATPs) or undergoes significant efflux/first-pass metabolism.",
    "The closest analog contexts are smaller and less polar; no human data exist for a dicyclohexyl barbiturate carboxylic acid.",
    "Species differences, such as penicillin V 18.9% in pigs versus 54.8% in human children, limit extrapolation."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "The query is a highly polar, fully ionized carboxylic acid with a barbiturate core and two cyclohexyl groups, and no direct F measurement is available. The physicochemical prior is low, but the available human records for structurally related polar acid/amide drugs all exceed F=20%, and L2 adds further supportive values from the same contexts. The only below-threshold L2 records are species-specific pig penicillin V data with low transferability. Therefore F >= 20% remains better supported than F < 20%, but confidence is low because the query is larger and more polar than the analogs and no direct query-specific evidence exists."
}

